            <ArticleTitle>Byproduct-Free Route to Aminosiloxane Monolayers on Silicon/Silicon Dioxide.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1639-1645</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.langmuir.6b04415</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The chemisorption of N-methyl-aza-2,2,4-trimethylsilacyclopentane from either the solution or the vapor phase produces monolayer films on silicon (oxide) substrates. The formation of a covalent siloxane linkage to the surface by this adsorbate is accompanied by ring opening, which produces no byproduct. The resulting secondary amine reacts with maleic anhydride to produce a carboxylic acid-terminated surface, accompanied by the formation of a stable amide bond. These reactions and their products were characterized by a combination of optical ellipsometry, contact-angle goniometry, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
